## Approach

We need to find the minimum number of multiplications required to multiply all the matrices.

Instead of trying every possible order repeatedly, we divide the problem into smaller parts. For every range of matrices from `i` to `j`, we try every possible position `k` where we can split the range into two parts.

For each split:

* Find the minimum cost to multiply the matrices from `i` to `k`.
* Find the minimum cost to multiply the matrices from `k+1` to `j`.
* Calculate the cost of multiplying these two resulting matrices.
* Take the minimum among all possible splits.

We store the result of each `(i, j)` range in the `dp` table. This prevents us from calculating the same range multiple times.

The recursion stops when `i == j`, because a single matrix does not need any multiplication.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
  private:
	int runner(vector<int>& nums, int i, int j, vector<vector<int>>& dp) {
		if(i == j) return 0;
		if(dp[i][j] != -1) return dp[i][j];

		int ans = 1e9;
		for(int k = i; k < j; k++) {
			// m1, m2, m3 ( n-1 )
			// 1   2    3
			// 0 1 2
			int cost1 = runner(nums, i, k, dp);
			int cost2 = runner(nums, k+1, j, dp);

			int mul_cost = nums[i-1] * nums[k] * nums[j];

			ans = min(ans, mul_cost+cost1+cost2);
		}
		dp[i][j] = ans;
		return ans;
	}
  public:
    int matrixMultiplication(vector<int> &arr) {
		int n = arr.size();

		int i = 1;
		int j = n-1;

		// when k = 1
		// 1,3 calls 1,1 and 2, 3
		// 2,3 calls 2,2 and 3,3

		// when k = 2
		// 2,3 calls 2,2 and 3,3
		vector<vector<int>> dp(n, vector<int> (n, -1));
		return runner(arr, i, j, dp);
    }
};

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n^3)

**Space Complexity:**
